00:34:45.949 --> 00:34:49.753
- Yeah, I mean, if you can't
debate it, it's not science.

00:34:49.753 --> 00:34:54.826
you know, the science is
rooted in reason and logic.

00:34:56.194 --> 00:34:59.530
And, you know, scientists
are supposed to be able

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:01.699 align:start size:84% position:16%
to subject themselves
to the, you know,

00:35:01.699 --> 00:35:03.868
to the furnace of debate,

00:35:05.236 --> 00:35:09.740
and, you know, allow their
ideas to triumph in the mud,

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:12.075
to become a kneeled debate,

00:35:12.075 --> 00:35:15.146
and then to triumph in rise
in the marketplace of ideas.

00:35:16.213 --> 00:35:18.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
And if you can't do
that, I mean, they say,

00:35:18.081 --> 00:35:21.752
well, because I'm a
fraud, and a quack,

00:35:21.752 --> 00:35:23.454 align:start size:88% position:13%
and a charlatan, and
all the other, you know,

00:35:23.454 --> 00:35:26.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
things that they say
about me that, you know,

00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:27.958
you can't debate
somebody like that.

00:35:27.958 --> 00:35:31.129
Well, that's a bunch of,
but first of all, you know,

00:35:31.129 --> 00:35:34.465 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've won hundreds
and hundreds of cases

00:35:34.465 --> 00:35:37.568
by arguing in front of juries.

00:35:37.568 --> 00:35:39.770
And, you know, with people who,

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:43.174
if I was making these kind
of, you know, absurd claims,

00:35:43.174 --> 00:35:45.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
and, you know, if I
was the kind of person

00:35:45.376 --> 00:35:49.713
that was making those kinda
claims that were baseless,

00:35:49.713 --> 00:35:51.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
that the other
attorneys in those cases

00:35:51.782 --> 00:35:54.285
would chew me up
and spit me out,

00:35:54.285 --> 00:35:56.354
and humiliate me in
front of the jury,

00:35:56.354 --> 00:35:59.656
'cause that's what
we are trained to do.

00:35:59.656 --> 00:36:04.095 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, you know, Hotez
has told the truth

00:36:04.095 --> 00:36:08.131
one time with Rogan,
two or three years ago,

00:36:08.131 --> 00:36:11.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
Rogan said, "Why don't
you debate Bobby Kennedy?"

00:36:12.636 --> 00:36:15.239
And he said, "Well, he's a
cunning lawyer, and you know,

00:36:15.239 --> 00:36:16.741
I'm not trained with debate."

00:36:16.741 --> 00:36:20.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, you know,
every scientist is,

00:36:20.677 --> 00:36:23.514
you look at what Darwin
did, and what, you know,

00:36:23.514 --> 00:36:28.586
and even like Archimedes
and, you know, all of the,

00:36:29.954 --> 00:36:33.224
Galileo and all these, they
had to defend their ideas.

00:36:33.224 --> 00:36:35.526
I mean, there are forums
set up all over the world

00:36:35.526 --> 00:36:38.128
where scientists get
together and, you know,

00:36:38.128 --> 00:36:39.297
and debate each other.

00:36:39.297 --> 00:36:42.499
And those debates are vicious.

00:36:42.499 --> 00:36:47.572 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they're oh, for them
very, very high stakes debate.

00:36:48.372 --> 00:36:50.841
And they use every trick,

00:36:50.841 --> 00:36:52.877
but that's part of
being a scientist

00:36:53.944 --> 00:36:56.213 align:start size:84% position:16%
is you take your ideas
and you give 'em.

00:36:56.213 --> 00:36:57.748
And you know, the way that,

00:36:58.582 --> 00:36:59.917
what I would do with Hotez

00:36:59.917 --> 00:37:02.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
is I really have a lot
of domain knowledge

00:37:02.252 --> 00:37:04.622
about vaccine studies.

00:37:04.622 --> 00:37:06.156 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I've written
books about them,

00:37:06.156 --> 00:37:07.491
I've assembled them.

00:37:08.893 --> 00:37:12.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I would, you
know, my way of debating

00:37:12.797 --> 00:37:15.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
would be asking him,
showing him the studies,

00:37:15.932 --> 00:37:18.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
and saying, you know,
"How can you explain this?

00:37:18.202 --> 00:37:21.639
If what you're saying is
true, show me your study."

00:37:21.639 --> 00:37:26.577 align:start size:97% position:3%
Either way, I've had
private debates with Hotez,

00:37:26.577 --> 00:37:29.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I've probably
spent, I don't know,

00:37:29.012 --> 00:37:31.415
10 or 20 hours with him,

00:37:31.415 --> 00:37:36.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
because a few years
ago somebody asked me,

00:37:38.522 --> 00:37:41.926
somebody who I was, with
whom I was very close,

00:37:42.827 --> 00:37:44.161
told me they were gonna come out

00:37:44.161 --> 00:37:46.063
publicly against me
with vaccination.

00:37:46.063 --> 00:37:48.332
And this person is
very wired in at NIH,

00:37:48.332 --> 00:37:51.269
and I said, "Before you do that,

00:37:52.069 --> 00:37:54.137
put me in a room with the guy

00:37:54.137 --> 00:37:57.074
who knows more about vaccines
than anybody in the world,

00:37:58.175 --> 00:38:00.143
and you listen to
me debate them,

00:38:00.143 --> 00:38:02.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then make up your
mind about whether or not

00:38:02.279 --> 00:38:04.181
you wanna go public against me."

00:38:05.215 --> 00:38:08.119
And so he called
Francis Collins,

00:38:09.186 --> 00:38:11.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Francis Collins
and Tony Fauci,

00:38:12.923 --> 00:38:14.591
and they didn't wanna
do it, but they said,

00:38:14.591 --> 00:38:17.027
"The guy to do it
is Peter Hotez."

00:38:17.027 --> 00:38:19.429
So he is their, you
know, their champion.

00:38:19.429 --> 00:38:22.099
He's their Goliath.

00:38:23.467 --> 00:38:27.471
And so Hotez did a series
of phone calls with me,

00:38:27.471 --> 00:38:32.543
and this other individual,
and let me just say this,

00:38:33.877 --> 00:38:36.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
the individual did not come
out publicly against me

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.382
after listening
to those debates.

00:38:38.382 --> 00:38:40.718
But we then had a lot
of email exchanges,

00:38:40.718 --> 00:38:42.653
where I would say,
"Show me the studies

00:38:42.653 --> 00:38:45.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
that shows that
vaccines don't cause,

00:38:45.589 --> 00:38:49.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
any of the vaccines given
the first six months of life,

00:38:49.626 --> 00:38:52.062
don't cause autism."

00:38:52.062 --> 00:38:55.766 align:start size:97% position:3%
He ended up sending me 11
studies about the MMR vaccine,

00:38:55.766 --> 00:38:58.769
which does not fit
in that category.

00:38:58.769 --> 00:39:00.470
And the Institute of Medicine,

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:03.641
the National Academy
of Sciences, those
studies don't exist.

00:39:03.641 --> 00:39:05.142
I showed him those papers.

00:39:06.209 --> 00:39:10.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, you know, I
don't think his fear

00:39:10.915 --> 00:39:15.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that I would say something
that was sort of crazy

00:39:15.086 --> 00:39:16.486
and detached from reality.

00:39:16.486 --> 00:39:20.224
I think his fear is that
I would expose reality.

00:39:21.325 --> 00:39:22.893
- Right, I mean,
that's the point.

00:39:22.893 --> 00:39:25.796 align:start position:0%
Like, it's not like you're
some random kook on the street.

00:39:25.796 --> 00:39:27.531
It's like if you were
saying crazy things,

00:39:27.531 --> 00:39:29.900
he should be able
to debunk them.

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:32.903
Let's hit a couple other topics.

00:39:32.903 --> 00:39:35.939 align:start position:0%
The affirmative action decision
came out a week or so ago

00:39:35.939 --> 00:39:37.408
from the Supreme Court.

00:39:37.408 --> 00:39:38.842
And you know, I've been on board

00:39:38.842 --> 00:39:41.277 align:start size:97% position:3%
so much of what you're
saying, even though as I said,

00:39:41.277 --> 00:39:44.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't consider myself a
Democrat, I moved to Florida.

00:39:44.547 --> 00:39:47.251
It's the first time I've ever
registered as a Republican

00:39:47.251 --> 00:39:49.253
since I've been here in Florida.

00:39:49.253 --> 00:39:51.021
But I saw your tweet thread

00:39:51.021 --> 00:39:52.756 align:start size:84% position:16%
about the affirmative
action decision.

00:39:52.756 --> 00:39:57.427
And basically you were
against the decision,

00:39:57.427 --> 00:39:59.329
and I was hoping maybe you
could just walk us through

00:39:59.329 --> 00:40:01.131
the philosophy there.

00:40:01.131 --> 00:40:05.202 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, and I understand
why people, you know,

00:40:05.202 --> 00:40:06.837
differ about that with me.

00:40:06.837 --> 00:40:09.006
And a lot of the people
who have supported me

00:40:09.006 --> 00:40:12.709 align:start size:88% position:13%
on other issues would
be disappointed by that.

00:40:12.709 --> 00:40:14.311
But you know, that...

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:20.651
Listen, I grew up in a state
that was a Jim Crow state.

00:40:21.552 --> 00:40:23.954
Oh, when I grew up in Virginia,

00:40:23.954 --> 00:40:28.459
I, you know, it was
illegal at that time

00:40:28.459 --> 00:40:30.628
for a Black man to
marry a white woman.

00:40:32.029 --> 00:40:36.066 align:start size:88% position:13%
Every aspect of
life was governed by
considerations of race.

00:40:36.066 --> 00:40:38.169 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, you were
identified by Black

00:40:38.169 --> 00:40:40.204
on your birth certificate,

00:40:40.204 --> 00:40:41.839
you were raised in a
Black neighborhood,

00:40:41.839 --> 00:40:44.208
you attended Black
segregated schools,

00:40:44.208 --> 00:40:48.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
you transportation,
public parks, everything,

00:40:48.145 --> 00:40:51.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
every aspect, you were
identified by race,

00:40:51.982 --> 00:40:55.519
on your death certificate and
buried in a Black cemetery.

00:40:55.519 --> 00:41:00.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
I had a guy, there was a guy
who worked for my family,

00:41:01.759 --> 00:41:03.426 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I, from when I was
young, I was going hunting,

00:41:03.426 --> 00:41:07.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
and trapping hawks down in
the southern part of Virginia.

00:41:07.797 --> 00:41:10.401
And he would carry
me on those trips.

00:41:11.535 --> 00:41:14.471
And he had been a
World War II vet.

00:41:14.471 --> 00:41:15.940
He was in the Seabees,

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:18.909 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was six foot five,
incredibly smart.

00:41:19.976 --> 00:41:21.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when we stopped
in restaurants,

00:41:21.444 --> 00:41:23.313
I would have to go in and buy
the food and bring it out,

00:41:23.313 --> 00:41:26.016 align:start size:88% position:13%
so that the two of us
could eat it in the car.

00:41:26.016 --> 00:41:28.285
He asked me one day
to accompany him

00:41:28.285 --> 00:41:32.656
into the local shoe store,

00:41:32.656 --> 00:41:34.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause he was not allowed
in the shoe store.

00:41:34.925 --> 00:41:38.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had to buy the shoes for
him, bring 'em outside,

00:41:38.795 --> 00:41:40.831
and he had to try
them on the curb.

00:41:40.831 --> 00:41:44.535
Oh, you know, when
I, and then my family

00:41:44.535 --> 00:41:47.671
was, you know, deeply involved
in the Civil Rights Movement,

00:41:47.671 --> 00:41:52.409
and you know, a lot of people
look at affirmative action

00:41:52.409 --> 00:41:56.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
and say, "Well, you
know, it's been 150 years

00:41:57.681 --> 00:41:59.316
since the Civil War.

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:02.019
You know, people have had,

00:42:02.019 --> 00:42:05.122
Black Americans have
had time to recover."

00:42:05.122 --> 00:42:07.791
But, you know, I was, you know,

00:42:09.159 --> 00:42:12.997
I saw that what it was like
to be Black when I was a kid.

00:42:12.997 --> 00:42:16.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
I saw that, you know,
that all of those

00:42:16.767 --> 00:42:19.236
higher levels of society
were forbidden, right,

00:42:19.236 --> 00:42:21.672
you know, in my lifetime.

00:42:21.672 --> 00:42:23.039
And my family was
deeply involved

00:42:23.039 --> 00:42:25.942 align:start size:84% position:16%
in ending that
system of Jim Crow.

00:42:25.942 --> 00:42:30.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
And part of, you
know, of that process

00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:33.416 align:start position:0%
was doing something that
normally I wouldn't believe in,

00:42:33.416 --> 00:42:38.489 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is allowing, you
know, considerations of race

00:42:42.192 --> 00:42:47.264
to affect the judgments
about who gets these,

00:42:48.064 --> 00:42:49.667
you know, positions in colleges.

00:42:49.667 --> 00:42:54.572 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I also know, Dave, at
almost all of those colleges

00:42:55.673 --> 00:42:57.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
already have a
system of preference,

00:42:57.541 --> 00:43:00.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the system of
preferences for legacies,

00:43:00.311 --> 00:43:01.712
you know, for people like me,

00:43:01.712 --> 00:43:04.648
who had a grandfather
who went to Harvard,

00:43:04.648 --> 00:43:07.083
a father went to Harvard,
uncles that went there,

00:43:07.083 --> 00:43:10.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so it's much easier
for me to get in.

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:15.225
But all of those legacies and
also the offspring of faculty

00:43:17.661 --> 00:43:18.862
are given preferences.

00:43:18.862 --> 00:43:22.232
And if you look at a
pie chart for who's in,

00:43:22.232 --> 00:43:25.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
almost none of those
kids are Black.

00:43:25.035 --> 00:43:27.971
So this is kind of a way
of counterbalancing that.

00:43:27.971 --> 00:43:30.307
And it's not, you
know, believe me,

00:43:30.307 --> 00:43:32.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have a lot of
problems with it too.

00:43:32.109 --> 00:43:35.011
And let me tell you
what the problems are

00:43:35.011 --> 00:43:37.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I see with
affirmative action is one,

00:43:37.914 --> 00:43:40.350
it's just contrary
to American values,

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:42.886 align:start size:84% position:16%
that, you know, that
were meritocracy.

00:43:42.886 --> 00:43:44.722
It's an illusion
though, as I say,

00:43:44.722 --> 00:43:46.156
that we're a meritocracy,

00:43:46.156 --> 00:43:48.993 align:start position:0%
'cause there are all these
built-in biases and preferences.

00:43:50.094 --> 00:43:52.363
But also I think
the bigger problem

00:43:52.363 --> 00:43:55.199
is what Barack Obama called,

00:43:57.067 --> 00:44:01.772 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, the subtle
bigotry of low expectations,

00:44:03.473 --> 00:44:07.878
that, you know, people,
a Black kid in a college,

00:44:07.878 --> 00:44:10.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a good college, that people
are looking at that kid,

00:44:10.481 --> 00:44:12.683
and saying, "Oh, he got in here

00:44:12.683 --> 00:44:14.718
because he got a preference."

00:44:14.718 --> 00:44:16.854
And I don't think that's
good for America either.

00:44:16.854 --> 00:44:19.322
So I understand that, you know,

00:44:19.322 --> 00:44:21.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think the Supreme
Court has made a decision

00:44:21.825 --> 00:44:25.595
that debate is now over
about affirmative action.

00:44:25.595 --> 00:44:29.232 align:start position:0%
We're moving on and we're
gonna, you know, try to preserve

00:44:29.232 --> 00:44:32.202
the beachhead that
Black Americans got

00:44:32.202 --> 00:44:34.737
during that period
in faculty positions,

00:44:34.737 --> 00:44:36.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
in business
positions, et cetera.

00:44:37.908 --> 00:44:41.244 align:start size:81% position:19%
But, you know, for
me, I would say

00:44:41.244 --> 00:44:43.680
that we probably should
have kept it in place

00:44:43.680 --> 00:44:47.918
for a few more years, but,
you know, it's over now.

00:44:47.918 --> 00:44:49.252
- Right, I get the intentions.

00:44:49.252 --> 00:44:50.687
Just one more on that,

00:44:50.687 --> 00:44:52.990
what would you say in
that case to the kid,

00:44:52.990 --> 00:44:55.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
the say 16 or
17-year-old Asian kid

00:44:55.759 --> 00:44:58.762
who, you know, scored
perfect on his SATs,

00:44:58.762 --> 00:45:00.630
and had all As, and, you know,

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:02.499 align:start size:84% position:16%
he knows he's not
getting into Harvard

00:45:02.499 --> 00:45:05.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
because someone with
significantly less grades

00:45:05.169 --> 00:45:06.370
is gonna get in.

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:07.771
No one gave that
Asian kid anything.

00:45:07.771 --> 00:45:09.673 align:start size:94% position:6%
He might be second
American from South Korea,

00:45:09.673 --> 00:45:11.274
and his parents owned a bodega,

00:45:11.274 --> 00:45:13.343
that was a lot of kids
that I grew up around.

00:45:13.343 --> 00:45:14.811
I mean, what do you
say to that kid?

00:45:14.811 --> 00:45:17.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that's what the nugget
really comes down to here.

00:45:17.481 --> 00:45:19.183
- Yeah, I agree with that.

00:45:19.183 --> 00:45:23.520
I think it's a, you know,

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:28.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's not an easy position
on any side to take,

00:45:28.125 --> 00:45:32.496
but you know, as far
as that Asian kid goes,

00:45:32.496 --> 00:45:34.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's kids getting
into that college

00:45:34.198 --> 00:45:35.933
because they're good at sports,

00:45:37.267 --> 00:45:40.737 align:start position:0%
who don't have that kind of
academic, you know, discipline,

00:45:40.737 --> 00:45:44.308
or academic performance record.

00:45:44.308 --> 00:45:46.743
So there's all kinds of biases

00:45:46.743 --> 00:45:50.080 align:start size:88% position:13%
that get certain
people in and you know,

00:45:51.815 --> 00:45:55.485
and it's not a merit, it's
not a straight meritocracy.

00:45:55.485 --> 00:45:57.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's no college, there
are very few colleges

00:45:57.921 --> 00:45:59.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
in this country, I'm
not gonna say no,

00:45:59.356 --> 00:46:02.259
that just look at your GPA,

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:05.128
and your, you know,
and your test scores,

00:46:05.128 --> 00:46:08.966
and say that the top,
you know, 3,000 kids

00:46:08.966 --> 00:46:10.400 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the best test
scores in the country,

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:12.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
the kids are gonna
get into this college.

00:46:12.770 --> 00:46:17.040
All the decisions are
dripping with bias

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:18.341
of one kind or another.

00:46:18.341 --> 00:46:23.413
So, and unfortunately
the bias, you know,

00:46:24.514 --> 00:46:25.982 align:start size:81% position:19%
these studies show
that the biases

00:46:25.982 --> 00:46:29.753
tend to reflect the
racial composition

00:46:29.753 --> 00:46:31.888
of the admissions department,

00:46:31.888 --> 00:46:34.191
that they, you know,
people tend to favor

00:46:34.191 --> 00:46:35.826
people who look like them,

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:38.161
and give them a preference.

